---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ArticleHero from '../../components/ArticleHero.astro';
import Card from '../../components/Card.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Hair Care Guide: Build a Routine That Works"
  description="Practical hair care guidance covering routine basics, hair types, and how to maintain color and treatments between salon visits."
  canonicalPath="/hair-care/"
>
  <ArticleHero eyebrow="Hair Care" title="Hair Care Guide" image="/images/gallery/gallery-esthetics-room-1.jpg" imageAlt="Private treatment room interior" />
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
    <p class="text-xs text-ink/50 mb-2"><a href="/" class="hover:underline">Home</a> / Hair Care</p>

    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Good hair care isn't about buying more products — it's about understanding
      what your specific hair and scalp actually need, and building a routine
      around that instead of a generic one-size-fits-all regimen. This guide covers
      the basics that apply broadly, with links to more specific guidance for
      curly hair, dry hair, colored hair, and general maintenance below.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Building a Basic Routine</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      A simple, effective routine usually covers four things: cleansing suited to
      your scalp, conditioning suited to your hair's porosity, weekly extra care
      (a mask or deep conditioner), and heat/UV protection when styling. Getting
      these four elements right matters more than any single "miracle" product —
      most hair care problems come down to a mismatch between the routine and the
      hair type, not a missing ingredient.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">How Often Should You Wash Your Hair?</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      There's no single right answer — oilier scalps often do better with more
      frequent washing (every 1–2 days), while dry or curly hair often looks and
      feels better washed less often (every 3–7 days). Adjust based on how your hair
      and scalp actually respond, not a fixed schedule. If you're changing your
      wash frequency, give it a few weeks to settle — scalps often overproduce oil
      temporarily when you first wash less often, which can feel counterproductive
      before it improves.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Understanding Your Hair's Porosity</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Porosity — how easily hair absorbs and retains moisture — affects which
      products actually work for you. Low-porosity hair tends to resist moisture
      and can feel weighed down by heavy products; high-porosity hair absorbs
      moisture quickly but loses it just as fast, and often benefits from
      heavier, sealing products. A simple way to get a rough sense of your
      porosity: a strand of clean, dry hair dropped in water that sinks quickly is
      likely higher porosity, while one that floats for a while is likely lower
      porosity. It's not a lab-grade test, but it's a useful starting point.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Preparing for a Salon Appointment</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-ink/80 mb-6">
      <li>Come with clean, product-free hair unless your stylist asks otherwise.</li>
      <li>Bring reference photos for cuts or color.</li>
      <li>Write down 2–3 questions you want answered during the consultation.</li>
      <li>Mention any recent chemical services (color, keratin, relaxer) even if it's been months, since it can affect how new services process.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">When to See a Professional Instead of DIY-ing It</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Most day-to-day hair care is well within the range of a good at-home routine.
      But certain things are worth handing to a professional rather than
      troubleshooting yourself — color correction, significant chemical damage,
      or any sudden change in hair texture or scalp health that doesn't respond to
      basic care. Persistent scalp irritation, unusual shedding, or anything that
      feels like it might be medical rather than cosmetic is worth a dermatologist
      visit rather than a salon appointment.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Adjusting Your Routine Seasonally</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Hair care isn't static — what works in a humid summer often needs
      adjusting once cold, dry winter air sets in, and vice versa. Many people
      need richer conditioners and less frequent washing in winter, when indoor
      heating dries out both skin and hair, and lighter, more frequent cleansing
      in summer, when humidity and sweat build up faster. Treating your routine
      as something to revisit each season, rather than a fixed set of products,
      tends to produce more consistent results year-round.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Explore More Hair Care Topics</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 mt-4">
      <Card title="Curly Hair Care" description="Moisture, definition and routine tips for curly hair." href="/hair-care/curly-hair/" image="/images/gallery/gallery-blonde-waves-1.jpg" imageAlt="Wavy, textured hair" />
      <Card title="Dry Hair Care" description="Rebuilding moisture and reducing breakage in dry hair." href="/hair-care/dry-hair/" image="/images/gallery/gallery-esthetics-room-1.jpg" imageAlt="Private treatment room" />
      <Card title="Caring for Colored Hair" description="How to keep color vibrant between salon visits." href="/hair-care/hair-coloring-care/" image="/images/gallery/gallery-blonde-waves-2.jpg" imageAlt="Vibrant hair color" />
      <Card title="Hair Maintenance" description="Everyday habits that keep hair healthy between cuts." href="/hair-care/hair-maintenance/" image="/images/gallery/gallery-private-studio-1.jpg" imageAlt="Private studio interior" />
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">A Routine That Grows With You</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      Your hair care needs today won't necessarily match what worked five years
      ago, or what will work five years from now — age, hormonal changes, and
      shifts in styling habits all mean a routine benefits from periodic
      reassessment rather than being set once and never revisited. Circle back
      to this page every so often and see whether the basics still fit.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
